@page "/attach"

<PageTitle>Attaching to your own EditForm</PageTitle>
<h1>Attaching to your own EditForm</h1>
<p>This form's <code>EditForm</code> is the page's own, not one Formidable renders — the "Submitted by" field is a plain <code>InputText</code> it already had, sitting beside a Formidable-managed expense list. <code>FormidableValidator</code> attaches to the cascaded <code>EditContext</code>; removing a line from the list is all it takes for that line's own error to leave the page, with nothing extra asked of the code that removed it.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "ExpenseReport.cs", "AttachMode.razor", "AttachMode.razor.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Submitted by</strong>: required. A plain <code>InputText</code>, not a Formidable one, so it registers for nothing on its own; the <code>FormidableFieldAnchor</code> beside it is the one line an unmigrated input needs. Committing a change engages the field, and the live channel answers for an engaged field with or without the anchor — what the anchor adds is the submit channel, which reveals only registered fields. Either way the message reaches the page through the compatibility bridge, Blazor's own <code>&lt;ValidationMessage&gt;</code>, rather than <code>FormidableFieldMessage</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Description</strong> (each expense line): required. A Formidable input: it registers with the field set exactly as it would under <code>FormidableForm</code>, and unregistering — a row leaving the list — is what the rest of this page demonstrates.</li>
            <li>The summary lists the blank line's error and the one on "Submitted by" alike: it reads the same channels the inline messages do, so an engaged field's live verdict reaches it on its own, and a submit's verdict reaches it for the fields something registered — the anchor registering the plain input the same way a Formidable input registers itself. Row identity follows the object instance, not the index, the same <code>@@key</code> convention <a href="/collections">Nested collections</a> uses.</li>
            <li>The page's own submit handler calls <code>FormidableValidator.ValidateForSubmitAsync()</code> rather than reaching through <code>Engine</code>, and that is what brings the rest of the submit story with it: a blocked submit lands the visitor on the first error here exactly as it does under <code>FormidableForm</code>, under the same <code>FocusFirstErrorOnInvalidSubmit</code> switch. What attach mode still does not resolve is where the fields <em>sit</em>: with no <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> of its own to observe, the summary lists issues in the engine's channel order, so "first" means first in that order rather than first down the page.</li>
            <li>The anchor speaks for disclosure, not for focus. The focus service addresses a field by a deterministic element id, and an input Formidable does not render carries none, so a focus aimed at "Submitted by" has nothing to land on until something supplies one. <code>FocusFallback</code> is the seam for exactly that: one callback here, wired to both the validator and the summary, renders the id and returns <code>true</code>, and the focus is retried once. It has a single miss to recover — the id it renders stays rendered, so every later focus aimed at the field lands first time. <a href="/vanilla">Vanilla interop</a> takes the other route and gives its native input that id from the start.</li>
            <li>If the built-in signal ever needs a nudge — reading <code>Engine</code> synchronously right after a mutation, ahead of the automatic reconcile's own continuation — <code>FormidableValidator</code> exposes <code>NotifyFieldSetChanged()</code> as an on-demand override. This page never calls it: removing a line is enough on its own.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Submit as seeded — the second line is blank, so its message appears beside it and in the summary.</li>
            <li>Remove that line — the message leaves both places; nothing else was asked of the page.</li>
            <li>Add a line and submit without filling it — the new row gets the identical treatment; remove it the same way.</li>
            <li>Clear "Submitted by" and tab out — its own error shows through the native <code>&lt;ValidationMessage&gt;</code> beside it on the tab alone, and the summary above lists it too: committing the change engages the field, and that is what discloses it.</li>
            <li>Submit while it is still empty — the submit blocks and the caret ends up back in "Submitted by", the first error the engine reports. Getting there took the page's <code>FocusFallback</code> the first time: nothing had rendered the id the focus service looks for, so that attempt missed and the fallback supplied one for the retry. The id stays from then on, so the caret lands the same way on every later submit. Fill the field back in and submit — the status line confirms.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<EditForm Model="_report" OnSubmit="HandleSubmit" id="@GateId" tabindex="-1">
    @* Everything that needs the cascaded FormidableFormContext — FormidableSummary and the row's
       own FormidableInputText/FormidableFieldMessage — has to be FormidableValidator's own
       ChildContent, not its sibling: with no ChildContent, FormidableValidator renders nothing
       at all, cascade included, rather than an empty wrapper. Context= names the typed
       fragment's parameter, because EditForm's own child content already claims the implicit
       "context" and the Razor compiler refuses to nest two claims on one name. What collides is
       the claim, not any use of it: the rename is owed even though nothing below reads the
       parameter, as nothing here does. *@
    <FormidableValidator TModel="ExpenseReport" @ref="_validator" FocusFallback="MakeSubmitterReachableAsync" Context="formidable">
        <div class="summary-slot">
            <FormidableSummary FocusFallback="MakeSubmitterReachableAsync" />
        </div>

        <div class="field">
            <label>Submitted by <InputText @bind-Value="_report.SubmitterName" id="@_submitterElementId" /></label>
            <ValidationMessage For="() => _report.SubmitterName" />
            <FormidableFieldAnchor For="() => _report.SubmitterName" />
        </div>

        <ul class="member-list">
            @foreach (var line in _report.Lines)
            {
                <li class="field" @key="line">
                    <label>Description <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="line.Description" /></label>
                    <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => line.Description" />
                    <div class="actions">
                        <button type="button" @onclick="() => RemoveLine(line)">Remove</button>
                    </div>
                </li>
            }
        </ul>

        <div class="actions">
            <button type="button" @onclick="AddLine">Add expense</button>
            <button type="submit">Submit</button>
        </div>
    </FormidableValidator>
</EditForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>
